00:14:00,281 --> 00:14:03,480
to innovate and have been for quite some time,

258
00:14:03,481 --> 00:14:07,160
including the use of robotics.

259
00:14:07,161 --> 00:14:09,960
The world's first mass produced industrial robot

260
00:14:09,961 --> 00:14:13,620
was introduced by American inventor George Duvall

261
00:14:13,621 --> 00:14:17,880
and made its debut in 1961 at a General Motors plant

262
00:14:17,881 --> 00:14:19,500
in New Jersey.

263
00:14:19,501 --> 00:14:23,240
The Unimate 1900 was the first autonomous robot,

264
00:14:23,241 --> 00:14:26,920
and it was used to spot weld car parts on an assembly line.

265
00:14:26,921 --> 00:14:30,080
Unimate stood for universal automation.

266
00:14:30,081 --> 00:14:34,680
The robotic arm could execute a variety of tasks,

267
00:14:34,681 --> 00:14:37,160
like die casting and welding, that

268
00:14:37,161 --> 00:14:40,760
would make manufacturing safer for the human workers.

269
00:14:43,560 --> 00:14:46,340
Industrial robotics would reshape production lines

270
00:14:46,341 --> 00:14:51,400
around the world, making everything from cars

271
00:14:51,401 --> 00:14:53,580
to motor graders.

272
00:14:53,581 --> 00:14:58,080
These robotic arms at John Deere can take care of welding

273
00:14:58,081 --> 00:15:01,480
that would be difficult for humans to do.

274
00:15:01,481 --> 00:15:04,960
They're also configured with AI that can find flaws

275
00:15:04,961 --> 00:15:08,360
and fix them, making sure the parts are ready

276
00:15:08,361 --> 00:15:12,560
for the next step of production.

277
00:15:12,561 --> 00:15:16,640
But not all heavy machinery manufacturers utilize robotics.

278
00:15:16,641 --> 00:15:20,440
In a world where everything from produce to parking

279
00:15:20,441 --> 00:15:23,880
is being automated, one of the world's top ranking global

280
00:15:23,881 --> 00:15:26,920
manufacturers produces without any automation.

281
00:15:29,620 --> 00:15:33,380
Started just over 30 years ago in 1989

282
00:15:33,381 --> 00:15:36,180
As a small welding supply company,

283
00:15:36,181 --> 00:15:39,960
Sani Heavy Industries relies on human hands

284
00:15:39,961 --> 00:15:43,260
to manufacture some of the world's best-selling excavators.

285
00:15:45,100 --> 00:15:47,960
The excavator has made itself indispensable

286
00:15:47,961 --> 00:15:49,760
on construction sites.

287
00:15:49,761 --> 00:15:52,700
It's a multi-talented utility machine

288
00:15:52,701 --> 00:15:55,660
that cuts, digs, or scoops heavy-duty materials.

289
00:15:57,080 --> 00:16:00,380
Without excavators, digging tunnels, pools, basements,

290
00:16:00,381 --> 00:16:02,400
or even pipes to run our water

291
00:16:02,401 --> 00:16:07,000
would be way more time consuming and expensive.

292
00:16:07,001 --> 00:16:09,800
And Sandy's excavators have turned the company

293
00:16:09,801 --> 00:16:15,380
into an $8 billion heavy equipment manufacturer.

294
00:16:15,381 --> 00:16:21,620
Today, we're at Sandy's newest $60 million, 272 acre US

295
00:16:21,621 --> 00:16:24,380
facility in Peachtree City, Georgia,

296
00:16:24,381 --> 00:16:30,020
to see how human hands built top selling excavators.

297
00:16:30,021 --> 00:16:31,920
The assembly team is currently working

298
00:16:31,921 --> 00:16:36,080
on one of Sandy's newest excavator models.

299
00:16:36,081 --> 00:16:40,500
The SY-225 excavator is a 54,000-pound excavator.

300
00:16:40,501 --> 00:16:43,260
It has a 21 and 1⁄2-foot dig depth,

301
00:16:43,261 --> 00:16:47,180
and it can pick up 28,500 pounds of material

302
00:16:47,181 --> 00:16:50,940
and be able to sling it every which way.

303
00:16:50,941 --> 00:16:54,880
And they're made right here.

304
00:16:54,881 --> 00:16:57,440
Everything is set here for each machine

305
00:16:57,441 --> 00:16:58,900
that we're making online.

306
00:16:58,901 --> 00:17:02,200
The variation allows us to build five different models

307
00:17:02,201 --> 00:17:06,100
of excavators on the same line with the same team members.

308
00:17:06,101 --> 00:17:08,900
Flexibility is the key to our success.

309
00:17:08,901 --> 00:17:11,520
The assembly team is attaching the tracks

310
00:17:11,521 --> 00:17:14,280
to the undercarriage, attaching the base

311
00:17:14,281 --> 00:17:17,320
to the top of the machine, and the attachment

312
00:17:17,321 --> 00:17:20,080
of additional components from hydraulics

313
00:17:20,081 --> 00:17:27,620
to various bucket sizes, all by human hands and no automation.

314
00:17:27,621 --> 00:17:31,220
they've found that they're able to adapt to changes quicker

315
00:17:31,221 --> 00:17:33,700
with their hands-on approach.

316
00:17:33,701 --> 00:17:36,400
That means each of these excavators

317
00:17:36,401 --> 00:17:39,740
is a U.S. workforce manufactured machine.

318
00:17:41,900 --> 00:17:43,600
Back at Davenport Works,

319
00:17:43,601 --> 00:17:46,840
John Deere has their own strong machine,

320
00:17:46,841 --> 00:17:51,840
weighing in at a whopping 125,000 pounds.

321
00:17:51,841 --> 00:17:53,840
Holy mo...

322
00:17:53,841 --> 00:17:55,680
What is this for?

323
00:17:57,620 --> 00:18:00,120
The biggest and most powerful machines

324
00:18:00,121 --> 00:18:03,500
include construction and farming tools,

325
00:18:03,501 --> 00:18:05,960
some of which have advanced harvesting our food

326
00:18:05,961 --> 00:18:09,940
from an all-day affair requiring several people,

327
00:18:09,941 --> 00:18:12,760
down to minutes with only one operator

328
00:18:12,761 --> 00:18:15,700
behind the wheel of heavy machinery.

329
00:18:15,701 --> 00:18:18,480
And here at John Deere's massive facility

330
00:18:18,481 --> 00:18:22,920
in Davenport, Iowa, we have the all-access pass

331
00:18:22,921 --> 00:18:25,780
to see how some of these giant machines come together,

332
00:18:25,781 --> 00:18:30,660
including John Deere's heaviest and biggest machine,

333
00:18:30,661 --> 00:18:34,780
the 944K Loader.

334
00:18:34,781 --> 00:18:38,960
Before road paving or any construction can begin,

335
00:18:38,961 --> 00:18:43,600
a job site needs to be cleared of debris, dirt, rubble,

336
00:18:43,601 --> 00:18:46,660
sometimes trees.

337
00:18:46,661 --> 00:18:51,540
That's what loaders like the 944K do.

338
00:18:51,541 --> 00:18:56,540
The team today is attaching the loader bucket to the machine.

339
00:18:56,541 --> 00:18:58,500
Holy.

340
00:18:58,501 --> 00:19:01,940
This is like when you go to the Natural History Museum

341
00:19:01,941 --> 00:19:05,160
and you finally see just how big a dinosaur was.

342
00:19:05,161 --> 00:19:06,920
This is for the 944K.

343
00:19:06,921 --> 00:19:09,540
It's the heaviest machine that John Deere built.

344
00:19:09,541 --> 00:19:10,580
This is the biggest?

345
00:19:10,581 --> 00:19:11,640
The biggest.

346
00:19:11,641 --> 00:19:13,020
This is it right here.

347
00:19:13,021 --> 00:19:14,640
Good gosh.

348
00:19:14,641 --> 00:19:16,800
The wheels are six feet high.

349
00:19:16,801 --> 00:19:18,260
The wheels alone are taller than me.

350
00:19:18,261 --> 00:19:20,180
I feel very inadequate.

351
00:19:20,181 --> 00:19:21,840
I want to introduce you to Donnie.

352
00:19:21,841 --> 00:19:23,140
He's one of our assemblers here.

353
00:19:23,141 --> 00:19:24,060
How do you do, Donnie?

354
00:19:24,061 --> 00:19:25,800
Nice to meet you. Good, good, good.

355
00:19:25,801 --> 00:19:29,140
Just the sheer size of some of these things is intimidating.

356
00:19:29,141 --> 00:19:30,420
It is absolutely intimidating.

357
00:19:32,840 --> 00:19:35,600
The standard bucket attachment for this machine

358
00:19:35,601 --> 00:19:40,080
alone weighs over 16,000 pounds.

359
00:19:40,081 --> 00:19:45,080
It's more than 13 feet wide and more than 6 feet tall.

360
00:19:45,081 --> 00:19:49,080
And it can move up to 10 cubic yards of heavy material.

361
00:19:50,180 --> 00:19:55,420
The bucket can basically pick up and move almost 1,000

362
00:19:55,421 --> 00:19:58,360
bowling balls in a single scoop.

363
00:19:58,361 --> 00:20:05,060
Overall, John Deere's 944K loader weighs 125,000 pounds.

364
00:20:05,061 --> 00:20:08,900
That's nearly as much as the space shuttle Endeavor.

365
00:20:08,901 --> 00:20:11,600
Even though this bucket is larger than life,

366
00:20:11,601 --> 00:20:15,500
attaching it requires utmost precision.

367
00:20:15,501 --> 00:20:16,500
No way.

368
00:20:20,180 --> 00:20:24,180
There's a little paint where the bushings meet,

369
00:20:24,181 --> 00:20:27,180
and the measurements are so precise

370
00:20:27,181 --> 00:20:29,180
that it will need to be ground down.

371
00:20:29,181 --> 00:20:33,180
We're dealing with the largest piece of heavy machinery

372
00:20:33,181 --> 00:20:37,180
John Deere makes, and something as minuscule as a layer of paint

373
00:20:37,181 --> 00:20:42,180
is hindering a small part from being attached properly.

374
00:20:42,181 --> 00:20:45,180
Measurements are precise enough that even a...

375
00:20:45,181 --> 00:20:46,180
That precise. Entire thing.

376
00:20:46,181 --> 00:20:47,180
The width of your hair.

377
00:20:47,181 --> 00:20:49,180
Wait, it's measured down to the width of your hair.

378
00:20:49,181 --> 00:20:50,680
to the width of your hair?

379
00:20:50,681 --> 00:20:51,680
Absolutely.

380
00:20:51,681 --> 00:20:53,520
That's nuts.

381
00:20:53,521 --> 00:20:55,520
Now that the paint has been removed,

382
00:20:55,521 --> 00:20:59,020
Donnie and his team are attaching this giant bucket

383
00:20:59,021 --> 00:21:02,860
to the 944K loader.

384
00:21:02,861 --> 00:21:05,940
I just got to ask, does no one ever get a ride in that?

385
00:21:05,941 --> 00:21:08,860
Does no one ever go up in the bucket?

386
00:21:08,861 --> 00:21:09,860
Not when I'm watching.

387
00:21:09,861 --> 00:21:10,860
No, I don't want to get it.

388
00:21:10,861 --> 00:21:11,860
Do you want to lie to it? Yes.

389
00:21:15,740 --> 00:21:18,300
In the pre-construction phase of projects,

390
00:21:18,301 --> 00:21:21,900
Loader buckets like the 944K work in tandem

391
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,700
with bulldozers and dump trucks.

392
00:21:24,800 --> 00:21:29,040
And the dump trucks can be just as big or even bigger.

393
00:21:29,140 --> 00:21:32,620
At 67 feet long and 26 feet tall,

394
00:21:32,720 --> 00:21:38,220
the Bellas 757-10 is about twice the size of a double-decker bus

395
00:21:38,320 --> 00:21:42,820
and multiple feet taller than Washington's nose on Mount Rushmore.

396
00:21:42,920 --> 00:21:46,660
It is the largest dump truck in the world,

397
00:21:46,661 --> 00:21:50,660
and it's used to mine natural resources like iron ore,

398
00:21:50,760 --> 00:21:53,060
which is the material used in everyday things

399
00:21:53,160 --> 00:21:55,160
like cars or appliances.

400
00:21:55,260 --> 00:21:58,880
It also mines copper, which makes wires for electronics

401
00:21:58,980 --> 00:22:01,880
and is the most common material in plumbing.

402
00:22:01,980 --> 00:22:05,640
And to do so, it needs more power than six Formula One

403
00:22:05,740 --> 00:22:10,580
race cars and is capable of carrying 450 metric tons.

404
00:22:10,680 --> 00:22:15,780
That's the equivalent of hauling 16 average fire trucks at once.

405
00:22:15,781 --> 00:22:20,620
This power comes with a $6 million price tag,

406
00:22:20,621 --> 00:22:22,580
so you most likely won't be seeing it

407
00:22:22,581 --> 00:22:25,920
at your local construction project.

408
00:22:25,921 --> 00:22:29,920
But not all heavy machinery operates on earthen soil.

409
00:22:29,921 --> 00:22:32,600
Some work in much slicker conditions,

410
00:22:32,601 --> 00:22:36,800
like the Zamboni Ice Resurfacer.

411
00:22:36,801 --> 00:22:39,500
The Zamboni Ice Resurfacer, funnily enough,

412
00:22:39,501 --> 00:22:43,400
actually came from Paramount, California in 1949.

413
00:22:43,401 --> 00:22:45,740
That's where Frank Zamboni and his brother

414
00:22:45,741 --> 00:22:48,540
owned and operated Iceland Skating Rink.

415
00:22:48,541 --> 00:22:51,240
It was one of the largest skating rinks of its time

416
00:22:51,241 --> 00:22:53,540
at 20,000 square feet.

417
00:22:53,541 --> 00:22:56,140
Maintaining a smooth ice surface

418
00:22:56,141 --> 00:23:00,080
where up to 800 skaters could gracefully glide

419
00:23:00,081 --> 00:23:03,220
required a tractor pulling a scraper

420
00:23:03,221 --> 00:23:05,780
while rink employees skated behind it

421
00:23:05,781 --> 00:23:07,820
and scooped up ice shavings,

422
00:23:07,821 --> 00:23:12,260
sprayed water onto the ice, and squeegeed the surface.

423
00:23:12,261 --> 00:23:13,920
It wasn't only labor-intensive.

424
00:23:13,921 --> 00:23:15,600
It took more than an hour.

425
00:23:15,601 --> 00:23:18,860
That is what inspired Frank Zamboni

426
00:23:18,861 --> 00:23:23,400
to build the Model A Zamboni Ice Resurfacer.

427
00:23:23,401 --> 00:23:26,440
It would shave the ice, remove the shavings,

428
00:23:26,441 --> 00:23:29,140
wash and squeegee the ice, and gather the shavings

429
00:23:29,141 --> 00:23:33,320
into a tank big enough to resurface the entire rink.

430
00:23:33,321 --> 00:23:36,320
It turned a job that would take multiple people over an hour

431
00:23:36,321 --> 00:23:39,280
into a job that one person could do in 10 minutes.

432
00:23:39,281 --> 00:23:43,420
By 1954, the Zamboni ice resurfacing machine

433
00:23:43,421 --> 00:23:47,580
saw its first use in an NHL game by the Boston Bruins,

434
00:23:47,581 --> 00:23:50,660
one of only six teams in the league at the time.

435
00:23:50,661 --> 00:23:54,320
Eventually, other teams followed suit across the league.

436
00:23:54,321 --> 00:23:56,700
In recent years, it's been reported that the company

437
00:23:56,701 --> 00:24:00,560
generates roughly $6 million or more per year.

438
00:24:03,340 --> 00:24:07,900
John Deere has been giving us a backstage pass

439
00:24:07,901 --> 00:24:10,860
to their enormous manufacturing facility

440
00:24:10,861 --> 00:24:15,780
to see how giant machines like dump trucks, motor graders,

441
00:24:15,781 --> 00:24:18,380
and backhoes come together.

442
00:24:18,381 --> 00:24:21,920
And a lot of it is thanks to some robotic help.

443
00:24:21,921 --> 00:24:24,140
All right, Adam, next we're going to go see some robots

444
00:24:24,141 --> 00:24:25,420
put on some tape.

445
00:24:25,421 --> 00:24:26,680
They look like they're alive.

446
00:24:26,681 --> 00:24:27,580
Oh, my gosh.

447
00:24:27,581 --> 00:24:29,380
This is like Tony Stark's laboratory.

448
00:24:29,381 --> 00:24:30,920
It is.

449
00:24:30,921 --> 00:24:34,160
After a while of watching the grace, fluidity,

450
00:24:34,161 --> 00:24:36,420
and precision of the robotic arms,

451
00:24:36,421 --> 00:24:39,460
Painting and priming this piece of a loader grader,

452
00:24:39,461 --> 00:24:44,920
it's actually quite a lot like a meticulously choreographed dance.

453
00:24:44,921 --> 00:24:45,920
It's like magic.

454
00:24:45,921 --> 00:24:47,660
It literally looks like magic.

455
00:24:47,661 --> 00:24:51,160
It's like a very fine-tuned mechanical ballet.

456
00:24:59,380 --> 00:25:03,620
That choreography can only happen once it's been programmed

457
00:25:03,621 --> 00:25:05,780
by skilled talents like John.

458
00:25:06,420 --> 00:25:09,420
Program it all to run with the conveyor.

459
00:25:09,421 --> 00:25:10,420
It is true.

460
00:25:10,421 --> 00:25:12,420
I didn't even think about that.

461
00:25:12,421 --> 00:25:15,420
It's not painting something that's standing still.

462
00:25:15,421 --> 00:25:17,420
This thing is in constant motion.

463
00:25:17,421 --> 00:25:21,420
So not only does it have to paint at prime

464
00:25:21,421 --> 00:25:24,420
and move around to make sure that each part is reached,

465
00:25:24,421 --> 00:25:27,420
but it's trying to hit a moving target.

466
00:25:28,420 --> 00:25:30,420
Is there like a John Deere yellow?

467
00:25:30,421 --> 00:25:33,420
We own a trademark for the combination

468
00:25:33,421 --> 00:25:35,420
and the usage of green and yellow together.

469
00:25:35,421 --> 00:25:38,420
OK, the iconic green and yellow.

470
00:25:38,421 --> 00:25:40,420
The green and yellow combination

471
00:25:40,421 --> 00:25:44,420
and farming equipment has been used by John Deere

472
00:25:44,421 --> 00:25:47,420
at least as far back as 1905.

473
00:25:47,421 --> 00:25:51,420
In fact, it is so distinguished, a recent court ruling

474
00:25:51,421 --> 00:25:55,420
cites the combo as a famous trademark.

475
00:25:55,421 --> 00:25:57,420
And no other company can use anything

476
00:25:57,421 --> 00:26:02,420
that closely resembles John Deere's iconic green and yellow duo.

477
00:26:02,421 --> 00:26:04,920
How much paint do you go through?

478
00:26:04,921 --> 00:26:07,420
46,000 gallons a year.

479
00:26:09,920 --> 00:26:13,060
The quintessential yellow is typically used

480
00:26:13,061 --> 00:26:15,660
as a safety measure in construction.

481
00:26:15,661 --> 00:26:20,480
It's a standout color that cries caution and visibility.

482
00:26:20,481 --> 00:26:23,140
Amid the brown and gray dust or debris

483
00:26:23,141 --> 00:26:26,020
that would typically surround a construction site,

484
00:26:26,021 --> 00:26:28,480
the shade of yellow emits wavelengths,

485
00:26:28,481 --> 00:26:31,040
which make it a highly visible color

486
00:26:31,041 --> 00:26:35,540
visible color to the human eye.

487
00:26:35,541 --> 00:26:38,740
And there's another iconic company producing construction

488
00:26:38,741 --> 00:26:42,920
vehicles in the same color, but meant for a different type

489
00:26:42,921 --> 00:26:45,560
of customer.

490
00:26:45,561 --> 00:26:49,600
I'm talking about Tonka toys.

491
00:26:49,601 --> 00:26:53,620
The Tonka truck actually dates back to 1946

492
00:26:53,621 --> 00:26:57,640
with the Mound Metal Craft Company from Mound, Minnesota.

493
00:26:57,641 --> 00:27:00,740
They first started making steam shovels and cranes.

494
00:27:00,741 --> 00:27:05,960
And within its first year, the company sold 37,000 units.

495
00:27:05,961 --> 00:27:08,240
After their initial success, the company

496
00:27:08,241 --> 00:27:13,220
changed their name to Tonka in 1955.

497
00:27:13,221 --> 00:27:17,120
The company was renamed Tonka after Lake Minnetonka

498
00:27:17,121 --> 00:27:19,020
in Minnesota.

499
00:27:19,021 --> 00:27:21,180
These toys were smaller than their real life

500
00:27:21,181 --> 00:27:24,940
counterparts, but they were still heavy.

501
00:27:24,941 --> 00:27:29,500
The early ones were made from auto-grade 20 gauge steel.

502
00:27:29,501 --> 00:27:33,800
The famous Mighty Dump Truck didn't come around until the mid-60s.

503
00:27:34,200 --> 00:27:38,540
When the Mighty Mighty Dump Truck came out, it really put Tonka on the map.

504
00:27:38,780 --> 00:27:41,560
It was heavy, it was durable, and for kids it was indestructible.

505
00:27:42,040 --> 00:27:44,800
It quickly became the company's best seller.

506
00:27:45,520 --> 00:27:50,820
It's estimated to have sold upwards of 15 million dump trucks over the years.

507
00:27:50,940 --> 00:27:57,720
As a young boy growing up on the farm, I had one of those original steel Tonka trucks that was indestructible.

508
00:27:57,840 --> 00:27:58,980
In fact, I still have it.

509
00:27:58,981 --> 00:28:00,640
It is my favorite toy.

510
00:28:00,641 --> 00:28:02,520
During its first year or so,

511
00:28:02,521 --> 00:28:05,180
it came with solid rubber tires,

512
00:28:05,181 --> 00:28:07,560
which were too heavy for kids.

513
00:28:07,561 --> 00:28:09,760
So the company removed some of the weight.

514
00:28:09,761 --> 00:28:11,460
The truck, even at 11 pounds,

515
00:28:11,461 --> 00:28:13,260
would be a heavy load for kids,

